# CoW 2G comprehension-loop sibling (OUT OF SCOPE — filed gap). The four
# comprehension emitters (list/string/dict/set) synthesize their own header/
# body/incr loops with NO save/restore, so an in-body materialize clone lands
# inside the re-executing body block — the same per-iteration throwaway by a
# different route. The 2G helper is statement-shaped and hooks lower_while/
# lower_for/lower_loop; comprehension bodies are synthesized exprs (un-scouted
# surface), so this is filed, not fixed here.
#
# LANE ASYMMETRY (Core #9): the self-host mirror hooks inside its lower_for_*
# bodies, which its comprehensions desugar THROUGH — so the SELF-HOST lane will
# likely fix these shapes FIRST, inverting the usual Rust-leads ordering.
#
# Asserts the CORRECT output: private copy [1,2,3,4], two drains → len 2, ys
# has 2 elements, caller stays 4. The compiler currently prints 3,2,4.
int drain_one(Vector[int] &v):
    v.pop()
    return v.len()

void f(Vector[int] xs):
    Vector[int] ys = [drain_one(&xs) for i in 0..2]
    print(xs.len())
    print(ys.len())

void main():
    Vector[int] a = [1, 2, 3, 4]
    f(a)
    print(a.len())
